		{
			"id": "ecUVYlyvNOSeXgHZj",
			"description": "Ghosthelm",
			"local_notes": "Worn by Aeldari psykers (Warlocks, Farseers, Spiritseers) beneath or in place of a helmet; does not stack with the Aeldari's own milder runic-warding limitation on Perils of the Warp (framework.md's \"Psykers and the Warp\" section) — it is an additional layer on top of that baseline, not a replacement for it.\n\nLore: A psychically-warded helm that partly shields its wearer's own mind from the backlash of channelling warp energy, worn by Aeldari psykers specifically to blunt the risk their own gift carries; Codex: Craftworlds background credits it with letting a Farseer or Warlock push their power further and more safely than they otherwise could, and it is best read as reducing the psyker's own Perils risk rather than granting any new power (no specific rules entry is cited).\n\nDesign: Modelled as a flat conditional bonus to the wearer's Perils of the Warp resistance roll, the same `conditional_modifier` feature the Imperium Psychic Hood already uses for a related but distinct effect (resisting an enemy's Warp Sorcery-tagged attack, not the wearer's own backlash) — per the assignment's own \"reduces Perils\" instruction, this entry targets the wearer's own Perils check specifically rather than reusing the Psychic Hood's wording verbatim, since the two items protect against different things even though both are small numeric wards against warp-related danger. +4 sits one step above the Psychic Hood's own +2, reflecting that a ghosthelm is a psyker's dedicated, personally-attuned item, not a generic command-squad relic. Handling: 2 lb, a light circlet or helm insert rather than full head armour — it carries no DR of its own; a psyker who wants physical head protection as well layers it under a standard Aspect or Guardian helmet.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Aeldari",
				"Psyker"
			],
			"tech_level": "10^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "6000",
			"base_weight": "2 lb",
			"features": [
				{
					"type": "conditional_modifier",
					"situation": "to resist Perils of the Warp checks",
					"amount": 4
				}
			],
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "ef67pFfKJr19XFBR6",
			"description": "Spirit Stone (Waystone)",
			"local_notes": "Worn on the breast by every craftworld Aeldari from a young age. On the wearer's death, the GM should rule the stone catches the departing soul rather than leaving it to Slaanesh outright, holding it until it can be laid to rest in a craftworld's Infinity Circuit (or, for an unusually gifted soul, drawn on for guidance or reincarnation as a Wraithguard/Wraithblade pilot) — an essentially guaranteed narrative outcome for a named character rather than a combat-time defence roll, since what the stone actually does happens after the character sheet's combat math is finished.\n\nLore: The single most universal piece of Aeldari wargear: every craftworld Aeldari wears a spirit stone from adolescence, meant to catch their soul the instant they die so it is not lost to Slaanesh, the \"Prince of Chaos\" that has hunted Aeldari souls since the Fall. Codex: Craftworlds background, Path of the Eldar, and every piece of Black Library Aeldari fiction agree on this without exception — it is not a rare relic but a basic, near-universal item of Aeldari life and death.\n\nDesign: The stone's real function (catching a departing soul) is a narrative death-and-afterlife mechanic GURPS's damage and defence system was never built to encode, so — following this project's own honest practice for the Null Rod and the Wraithcannon's warp-rift effect — it is written as rules text for the GM to apply at the moment of death rather than invented as a combat statistic. It carries no combat bonus: the stone itself catches the soul automatically at death, and a Warlock or Spiritseer only retrieves it afterwards. It is a common item, worn by every craftworld Aeldari from adolescence, so it is priced as one ($300 for a wraithbone stone and its setting), not as a relic. Handling: 0.25 lb, a small worn stone rather than a weapon or armour piece — it carries no ST requirement, DR or Weak Points rating.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Aeldari"
			],
			"tech_level": "12^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "300",
			"base_weight": "0.25 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "e6h1DVyogBnu89UdQ",
			"description": "Swooping Hawk Wings",
			"local_notes": "No flat Move bonus is written on the sheet, because the wings only help in bursts. While airborne and using the wings actively the wearer moves at Basic Move +4 for a burst of a few seconds to a few dozen yards before the grav-plates need to recharge (GM applies it by hand, on the lines of the Jet Pack rules, B287) — treat as a short powered leap/glide, not sustained flight, and require a DX or Acrobatics roll to land cleanly rather than tumble. Once per fight (GM's discretion on recharge), the wearer may add a single \"Skyleap\": an extra +2 to Basic Move and immunity to falling damage for that one jump only, representing the wings' full-power vertical leap Swooping Hawks are shown using to clear an obstacle or a firing line entirely.\n\nLore: Worn by Swooping Hawk Aspect Warriors, a grav-assisted wing harness that lets them leap and glide across a battlefield far faster than any foot soldier, striking from above before leaping clear again; Codex: Craftworlds background and Path of the Eldar both depict Swooping Hawks as skirmishers who are never on the ground for long, using bursts of grav-lift rather than continuous flight.\n\nDesign: Modelled on GURPS's Jet Pack rules (B287) rather than a bespoke flight mechanic, since a Swooping Hawk's short, bursting grav-leaps behave much like one (the wings are grav-plates and a wing frame, not a jump pack) — a Basic Move bonus (+4, comparable to Enhanced Move (Ground) 1's own effective speed increase, B52) usable in bursts rather than sustained and so applied by the GM in play instead of as a permanent attribute on the sheet, with the powered vertical \"Skyleap\" the assignment implicitly calls for (Swooping Hawks are consistently shown clearing terrain or enemy lines in one bound) written as a once-per-fight special option rather than a constant bonus, since a repeatable no-cost extreme leap would make ground obstacles and falls meaningless for the rest of the fight. Handling: 18 lb for the grav-plate harness and wing frame, heavier than any armour piece in this file since it's a powered device rather than passive protection, but light enough for an Aspect Warrior to fight in rather than merely fly with.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Aeldari"
			],
			"tech_level": "11^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "12000",
			"base_weight": "18 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eAsCfB3A0xmIEjJh6",
			"description": "Warp Spider Jump Generator",
			"local_notes": "Once per turn, the wearer may make a short, involuntary-looking blink through the warp instead of a normal move: this is the Teleport advantage (B90), range 1d×3 yards in any direction the wearer can see, including through solid obstacles up to a few yards thick. Each use requires a roll against HT-2 (a non-psychic risk specific to this device, not the psyker's own Perils of the Warp check): failure means the jump goes short, wide, or (on a critical failure) leaves the wearer Stunned for 1 turn as they stagger out of the warp in the wrong place, at the GM's discretion, per the Teleport advantage's own risk notes.\n\nLore: Worn by Warp Spider Aspect Warriors, a jump generator that blinks its wearer bodily through the immaterium for an instant, appearing yards away in the blink of an eye; Codex: Craftworlds background and Path of the Eldar both treat this as a genuinely dangerous device — the fiction shows the occasional Warp Spider lost to a jump gone wrong, not merely a safe teleport with a cool visual — and the Warp Spiders' own shrine motto reflects a body of warriors who have made peace with that risk.\n\nDesign: Built as an honest short warp-teleport per the assignment's own instruction, reusing this project's existing Displacer Field entry in fields.yaml as its design basis (the Teleport advantage, B90) rather than inventing a new mechanic, since both devices do the same basic thing (bodily short-range teleportation) even though a Displacer Field triggers defensively and this triggers at the wearer's own choice as a movement action. Where the Displacer Field is a rare, safe Aeldari-derived relic in Imperial hands, the Warp Spider generator is worn routinely by its own Aspect Warriors and so needs its own real, regularly-faced risk to match the lore's \"a jump gone wrong\" element — modelled as a flat HT-2 roll each use rather than a full Perils of the Warp check, since the risk here is mechanical/warp-transit failure, not a psychic backlash the wearer's own mind is generating. Handling: 15 lb for the generator and its power cell, worn as a backpack unit; carries no DR, ST requirement or weapon stats of its own.",
			"tags": [
				"Xenos",
				"Aeldari"
			],
			"tech_level": "12^",
			"legality_class": "0",
			"base_value": "15000",
			"base_weight": "15 lb",
			"quantity": 1
		},
		{
			"id": "eSuAkA_frHUYjc_X2",
			"description": "Holo-Suit",
			"local_notes": "The Dodge bonus applies only against attacks the wearer can see coming (it scatters the wearer's visual image, not their actual position) and does not stack with a shield's own Defense Bonus; against attacks the wearer cannot see or sense at all (a surprise attack, an attack from an undetected direction), it grants no bonus. A Harlequin's own inhuman grace (Aeldari reflexes, B14's Combat Reflexes and similar traits) stacks with it normally.\n\nLore: Worn by every Harlequin, a holo-field/domino-field suit that scatters and multiplies the wearer's visible image into a shifting blur of colour and false afterimages; Codex: Craftworlds background and every Harlequin-focused depiction in the fiction treat it as making a Harlequin extraordinarily hard to actually hit despite wearing no real armour at all, in keeping with the Harlequins' whole fighting philosophy of not being where the blow lands rather than shrugging the blow off. framework.md's own Aeldari anchor is explicit: \"large defence bonus from scattering the wearer's image, not DR.\"\n\nDesign: Modelled as a flat Dodge bonus via the `attribute_bonus` feature rather than as DR, per the anchor's own explicit instruction — this is the same \"avoidance, not resistance\" design principle this project already uses for the Imperium's Displacer Field (Teleport instead of DR), applied here to a defence-roll bonus instead of an escape mechanic, since a holo-suit's lore effect is about not being hit rather than about escaping after being hit. +3 Dodge is a large, singular bonus deliberately — comparable to a good Combat Reflexes-and-Enhanced- Dodge stack on its own — reflecting that this is meant to be the defining defensive trait of every Harlequin who wears one, not a minor perk, while the \"only against attacks the wearer can see\" limitation keeps it from being an unconditional, no-downside upgrade over real armour. Handling: 8 lb for the suit and its holo-field generator; carries no DR or Weak Points rating at all, the clearest possible contrast in this file to the rigid- and flexible-DR armour entries above it.",
